                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design the programme, choose the partnership & price`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the flagship and pick a public-private partnership archetype, each with its own control-versus-value trade-off. A <strong>blended</strong> model keeps your brand and curriculum while co-creating with anchor employers; a <em>white-label platform</em> scales fastest but cedes curriculum control and 30–40% of revenue. Price against SkillForge — premium-but-credible, head-on match, or full legacy price. Then allocate the <strong>€4.5M seed</strong> across content reskilling, technology, faculty and marketing — and <strong>keep a contingency</strong>. Spend every euro and the board will ask what funds Year 2.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Reskill faculty & defend the core under shock`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `A mid-launch shock: <strong>22 of 95 faculty</strong> resist the new model, the EdTech partner demands curriculum control as a condition of scale, and an anchor employer offers 80 guaranteed seats a year for exclusivity. Capability — not strategy — is usually the binding constraint: a blueprint with no one to deliver it is a brochure. Reskill the willing and hire for true gaps, decide how to answer the partner's control demand, choose whether to grant exclusivity, and protect degree-master quality and morale <em>without letting the legacy core veto the future</em>.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Launch, measure & make the case to the board`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present a four-minute case to the foundation chair. Commit to an <strong>innovation scorecard</strong> — leading indicators (partnership value, pipeline) now, lagging ones (enrolment, contribution, revenue mix) later. Show how the business funds itself beyond the seed: bridge the <strong>month-14 break-even</strong> and reinvest surplus, a three-year path off 62% degree-revenue dependence. And answer the chair's hardest question: <em>what does the school stop doing</em> to fund the future? Defend a separate, durable ecosystem over a fast paper win.`,

