                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_separate:      `<strong>That is the Christensen-correct response.</strong> A low-end disruptor is not beaten by a better version of the thing it is undercutting. You are building a separate, fit-for-purpose engine for the segment that never applied to you — now choose which segment makes it credible.`,
                    coach_r1_polish:        `<strong>You have decided to polish the legacy core.</strong> A better full-time MBA over-serves customers who are not leaving while ignoring the 14,000 who never applied to you. Applications are still down 11% and SkillForge just signed your second-largest client. Where is your separate engine for the disrupted segment?`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Waiting is a decision — the wrong one.</strong> Prestige and accreditation did not stop SkillForge signing 14,000 learners in 18 months. Low-end disruptors are dismissed right up until they move upmarket into your core. By the time it is obvious, it is too late.`,
                    coach_r1_acquire:       `<strong>A price war on the disruptor's turf is unwinnable.</strong> SkillForge runs at a fifth of your cost base with venture money behind it. You cannot out-cheap a platform; you can out-relevance it with an ecosystem it cannot replicate.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp where-to-play.</strong> A separate engine aimed squarely at mid-career reskillers — the fast-growing segment SkillForge is winning — is exactly the move. You are not defending the past; you are building the relevant future.`,
                    coach_r1_everyone:      `<strong>Refusing to choose funds three half-programmes.</strong> With €4.5M you cannot serve new graduates, mid-career reskillers and senior executives credibly at once. Which single segment makes the flagship credible — and which two are you deliberately not serving yet?`,
                    coach_r1_noassumption:  `<strong>You have not stress-tested the strategy.</strong> Name the one assumption that, if wrong, breaks the whole plan — that the segment exists, that it will pay, that you can deliver — and test it now, while it is cheap to be wrong.`,
                    coach_r2_blended:       `<strong>A blended model is the durable choice.</strong> Anchor employers co-create real value while you keep brand and curriculum, and selective EdTech delivery gives you reach without surrendering control. That is orchestrating the ecosystem rather than being absorbed by it.`,
                    coach_r2_academy:       `<strong>A pure corporate academy buys certain enrolment at a price.</strong> Low margin and dependence on a few employers tie your relevance to their hiring cycles. It is a useful anchor — but make sure it is one node in an open ecosystem, not the whole programme.`,
                    coach_r2_platform:      `<strong>You will be live by autumn — but at what cost?</strong> White-labelling hands curriculum control and 30–40% of revenue to a platform that is SkillForge's cousin. In three years, whose brand do learners trust, yours or the platform's? You scaled fast and disintermediated yourself.`,
                    coach_r2_consortium:    `<strong>A public consortium brings subsidised seats and legitimacy</strong> — but it is slow and compliance-heavy, and the public-reporting obligations will outlast the launch. Useful for scale and access; weak as the sole engine of a flagship that must move fast.`,
                    coach_r2_alone:         `<strong>Building alone keeps every euro of control and margin</strong> — and is the slowest, most capital-hungry path with €4.5M. You forgo the co-created value an ecosystem provides. Relevance here comes from partners, not from doing everything yourself.`,
                    coach_r2_pricematch:    `<strong>Matching SkillForge starts a price war you lose.</strong> At a fifth of your cost base the platform wins on price every time. Compete on ecosystem outcomes and accreditation — premium-but-credible — not on being the cheapest.`,
                    coach_r2_priceluxury:   `<strong>Full legacy-master price ignores the disruptor.</strong> Pricing the flagship as elite prices it out of the very mid-career segment you chose to serve. The point is relevance to that segment, not protecting your old margin.`,
                    coach_r2_spendall:      `<strong>Every euro is spent — so what funds Year 2?</strong> The first cohort breaks even in month 14. With no contingency you have shown a launch, not a business. Leave a bridge to break-even and a logic that reinvests the surplus.`,
                    coach_r2_underfund:     `<strong>You have left too much on the table.</strong> A large contingency is prudence taken to the point of starving the launch — too little content, technology and go-to-market to make the flagship credible. Fund the launch properly, then keep a sensible bridge.`,
                    coach_r2_thincontent:   `<strong>You under-funded content and faculty reskilling.</strong> The flagship's relevance lives in the curriculum and the people who teach it. Starve that line and you ship a brochure with good marketing behind it.`,
                    coach_r3_build:         `<strong>Build the willing, buy for the gaps.</strong> Funded time and incentives turn resisting faculty into carriers of the new model, and selective hiring fills the true capability gaps. That is how a blueprint becomes a programme someone can actually deliver.`,
                    coach_r3_buyonly:       `<strong>Buying only leaves your institution behind.</strong> Hiring adjuncts while the 95 core faculty stay untouched builds a parallel programme that never embeds — and the legacy core, unreskilled, quietly resists. Capability has to spread inward, not bypass the institution.`,
                    coach_r3_mandate:       `<strong>A mandate with no protected time breeds paper compliance.</strong> Ordering all 95 to teach new content without funded time or incentive hardens the 22 resisters and burns out the willing. You get a programme on paper that nobody owns.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>A blueprint with no one to deliver it is a brochure.</strong> Funding the plan but not the people is the classic error — 22 faculty are quietly refusing and your best adjuncts could leave for SkillForge. Capability, not strategy, is the binding constraint here.`,
                    coach_r3_shared:        `<strong>Co-governance is the move.</strong> A joint board with clear IP terms lets you scale through the partner while protecting the core and the brand — shared control, not surrendered control. You keep what is defensible and still move fast.`,
                    coach_r3_cede:          `<strong>Ceding control for speed disintermediates you.</strong> Hand the platform the curriculum and 38% of revenue and in three years learners trust the platform, not the school. Speed bought at the price of your own relevance is a poor trade.`,
                    coach_r3_exclusivity:   `<strong>Exclusivity trades an ecosystem for a captive contract.</strong> 80 guaranteed seats feel safe, but no rival employer will now co-fund the programme and your relevance is tied to one company's hiring cycle. Is that an ecosystem or a dependence?`,
                    coach_r3_overprotect:   `<strong>Defending the core too hard lets it veto the future.</strong> Pouring all your attention into protecting the degree masters starves the flagship of the leadership focus it needs in launch year. Protect quality and morale — without handing the legacy a veto.`,
                    coach_r3_noprotect:     `<strong>Neglecting the paying core is just as dangerous.</strong> The degree masters still fund 62% of the school while the flagship is unproven. Let quality and morale collapse there and you lose the cash that funds the very transition you are building.`,
                    coach_r4_leadlag:       `<strong>Leading plus lagging indicators is the right scorecard.</strong> Partnership value and pipeline tell you early whether the flagship is winning; enrolment, contribution and revenue mix prove it later. You will see decay before it shows up in the headline number.`,
                    coach_r4_enrolonly:     `<strong>Headcount alone is a vanity metric.</strong> Reporting enrolment each term tells the board nothing about ecosystem health, contribution, or the path off 62% dependence — and it is the easiest number to flatter while the business hollows out.`,
                    coach_r4_launchpr:      `<strong>A launch-week press dashboard is exactly how innovations decay.</strong> The headlines fade, attention moves to the next priority, and with no enduring measurement the flagship slides back. Measurement has to outlive the launch.`,
                    coach_r4_reinvest:      `<strong>That is a business, not just a launch.</strong> Bridging the month-14 break-even and reinvesting surplus into the ecosystem is the three-year path off 62% degree dependence the chair is pressing you for. You have shown how Year 2 funds itself.`,
                    coach_r4_spendall:      `<strong>A strong launch with no Year-2 bridge is half a plan.</strong> The first cohort breaks even in month 14 — what carries the business across that gap, and what reinvests the surplus? Spending it all now is the error the board is watching for.`,
                    coach_r4_subsidy:       `<strong>Assuming a recurring subsidy is wishful financing.</strong> Public funding is slow, conditional and politically reversible. Build a model that stands on its own contribution and treat any subsidy as upside, not as the foundation.`,
                    coach_r4_addon:         `<strong>Running the flagship on top of everything funds nothing.</strong> The chair asked what you stop doing — 'stop nothing' spreads scarce faculty and budget across a portfolio you cannot all sustain. Focus is funded by sunsetting what no longer earns its place.`,
                    coach_r4_cutexec:       `<strong>Cutting exec-ed kills the cash that funds the transition.</strong> The exec-ed line still funds 30% of the income you need during launch year. Stopping the future's own bridge to fund the future is self-defeating — sunset the sub-scale, not the profitable.`,

